- `action(n3)` to `action(n4)`: Omits the documented step "the Faculty will choose the design" between sending the design and identifying content for clearance. (SMC1)
- `action(n6)`: Has no outgoing `precedence` statement, creating a dead-end; the source explicitly states "then the process should be continued." (SMC3, SMC5)
- `action(n12)` to `end(e2)`: Terminates the reconsideration path prematurely, whereas the source states "then the process should be continued," indicating a loop-back or subsequent workflow rather than completion. (SMC5)
- `actor(a_default, "Default", [...])`: Assigns all process nodes to a generic actor, ignoring distinct responsibilities assigned to "Faculty" (choose design) and "MPC" (calculate cost, add margin, notify Bookstore) in the source. (SMC7)
- `branch(b1, XOR)` and `branch(b2, XOR)`: Lack corresponding `guard(...)` statements to evaluate `v1` and `v2`; reliance on `label(...)` tags fails to enforce the exclusive decision logic described. (SMC4)
- `action(n1, "start the process")`: Hallucinates an initialization step not present in the description; the process begins directly with sending the Course Packet Information. (SMC1)
- `defines(v1, ..., {})` and `defines(v2, ..., {})`: Declare empty value ranges `{}` instead of defining valid decision values (e.g., `{true, false}` or clearance/status strings), rendering them semantically inert. (SMC6)
- `action(n5)` and `action(n9)`: Incorrectly model conditional checks as mandatory sequential tasks preceding branches, whereas the source presents them as decision criteria governing path selection, violating proper control-flow semantics. (SMC4)

